= SP800-90B Entropy Measurements <sec:measurements>

The following table presents the SP800-90B entropy measurements indicating
whether the found entropy is sufficiently high to support the entropy analysis
given in section @sec:90b-315. Entropy values are given in bits and apply to
the entropy found in one time stamp generated when receiving an interrupt
event. The testing shown in this section provides the quantiative foundation
of the entropy analysis compliant to sections @sec:90b-3151 as well as all
other assessments required for SP800-90B.

The testing collected raw unconditioned time stamps as delivered by the file
`esdm_raw_hires` of the raw entropy interface of earlier versions of the
interrupt entropy source; the measurements are kept as the characterization
of the noise source (section @sec:noise-source). The kernel module of today
provides that file as `/sys/kernel/debug/esdm_es/esdm_raw_hires`, recording
time stamp differences (section @sec:ig718-testing). The entropy calculation
is based on 1,000,000 raw time stamps collected by the ESDM. To speed up the raw
time stamp collection as well as to obtain a worst-case assessment, all test
systems were either ping-flooded or within an SSH-session a `find /` was
executed to generate a large number of interrupts in a short amount of time.
The ping-flood generator was in close network proximity (e.g. KVM host, or a
system at most one switch away from the test system).

The entropy result listing in the table below is generated as follows. The
time stamps generated by the ESDM for each interrupt event is extracted and
concatenated to form a bit-stream. This bit stream is processed by the
#link("https://github.com/usnistgov/SP800-90B_EntropyAssessment/")[NIST
SP800-90B entropy analysis tool] to obtain an entropy rate. This entropy rate
is listed below. The 8 least significant bits (LSB) of the time stamp were
used, the entropy rate applies to those 8 data bits. As discussed in sections
@sec:90b-3151, the kernel module credits each time stamp with
$1 \/ #raw("ESDM_ES_OSR")$ bit of entropy, a third of a bit by default. As
all values in the table below show significantly more entropy even with the
worst-case measurement of 8 LSB, the ESDM underestimates the entropy existing
in the respective system. Thus, the ESDM is considered to operate securely on
these systems. The test complies with SP800-90B outlined in section
@sec:90b-313.

